  "material": "oil on canvas",
  "dimensions": "43.2 × 63.5 cm",
  "production_date": "1713-14",
  "brief_description": "This is a modello (a preparatory sketch of an entire composition) for a large picture commissioned to celebrate the marriage of Johann Wilhelm, Elector Palatine, and Anna Maria Luisa de' Medici, daughter of Cosimo III de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany, which had taken place in 1691.\n\nThe groom, dressed in Roman costume with a plumed helmet, is surrounded by mythological figures. The goddess Juno blesses the marriage flame, tended by Hymen, god of marriage. The bride approaches from the left, her head lowered to suggest modesty and subservience, both seen as wifely virtues.\n\nIn 1713--14 Pellegrini worked on a series of 14 pictures that celebrated Johann's life and reign, which were intended for Bensberg Palace, near Düsseldorf. This series (now in the Schleissheim Palace, near Munich) is generally considered his most important work. Pellegrini travelled extensively throughout his career and in doing so established himself as one of the most sought-after decorative painters in Europe during the early eighteenth century.",
